#~
A collection leaves an Int[] element alone even when its value is an object's
address (GC roots phase 6, docs/GC_ROOTS_DESIGN.md section 10).

An Int[] and an object array used to share one heap tag (INT_TYPE), so the
collector scanned every Int[] element as a possible reference and relocated
any element equal to a forwarded young object's address: an Int that held an
address changed value across a minor collection. The compiler now allocates
an Int[] with NEW_INT_ARY, tagged INT_ARY_TYPE, which the collector never
scans, and an object array with NEW_OBJ_ARY.

Runtime->TestAddressOf(o) hands back an object's address as an Int; the VM
returns 0 for it unless OBJECK_GC_TEST_HOOKS=1, so only run_vm_flag_tests.py
runs this file, with the variable set. The address goes into an Int[] element
while Churn() allocates enough to collect, and is compared with the same
value kept in a typed Int local, which a collection never touches. The
negative control is OBJECK_GC_ROOTS=conservative, under which the collector
scans an Int[] the old way: it must FAIL there and PASS by default.
~#

class Box {
  @v : Int;

  New(v : Int) {
    @v := v;
  }

  method : public : Get() ~ Int {
    return @v;
  }
}

class GcAddressIntArray {
  function : Main(args : String[]) ~ Nil {
    probe := Box->New(1);
    if(Runtime->TestAddressOf(probe) = 0) {
      "FAIL: Runtime->TestAddressOf returned 0; run with OBJECK_GC_TEST_HOOKS=1"->PrintLine();
      Runtime->Exit(2);
    };

    bad := Run();
    if(bad = 0) {
      "PASS: no Int[] element holding an object's address was rewritten by a collection"->PrintLine();
    }
    else {
      "FAIL: {$bad} Int[] element(s) holding an object's address changed by a collection"->PrintLine();
      Runtime->Exit(1);
    };
  }

  function : Run() ~ Int {
    bad := 0;
    for(i := 0; i < 2000; i += 1;) {
      o := Box->New(i);
      held := Int->New[2];
      held[0] := Runtime->TestAddressOf(o);
      before := held[0];
      Churn(i);
      if(held[0] <> before) {
        bad += 1;
      };
    };
    return bad;
  }

  function : Churn(n : Int) ~ Int {
    s := 0;
    for(j := 0; j < 200; j += 1;) {
      b := Box->New(j);
      s += b->Get();
    };
    return s;
  }
}
